  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Choosing a name your cat will answer to",
      "content": "<ul><li><strong>Keep it short</strong>, or have a short version: one or two syllables are easiest to call. Lady Arabella can be Bella at teatime.</li><li><strong>Make it different</strong> from the names of other pets and people in the house.</li><li><strong>Use it for good things.</strong> Say the name before food, treats and fuss, not when you're shooing them off the worktop.</li></ul><p>Cats do learn their names: a 2019 study in <em>Scientific Reports</em> found pet cats react to their own name more than to similar words. Coming when called is up to them.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Naming by coat colour",
      "content": "<ul><li><strong>Ginger cats</strong> are more often male, because the ginger gene is carried on the X chromosome. That's why ginger boy names are easier to find than ginger girl names.</li><li><strong>Tortoiseshell and calico cats</strong> are almost always female for the same reason. A tortie tom is rare.</li><li><strong>Black and white cats</strong> are often called tuxedo cats, which is where names like Tux, Domino and Gatsby come from.</li></ul>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What is the most popular boy cat name in the UK?",
      "answer": "Milo was the highest male name in Go.Compare's list of cat names for 2025, second only to Luna overall. Simba, Loki, Leo and Charlie also made the top ten."
    },
    {
      "question": "Why are most ginger cats boys?",
      "answer": "The gene for ginger fur is carried on the X chromosome. A male cat needs only one copy to be ginger, while a female needs two, so ginger toms are much more common than ginger queens."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can you rename a cat?",
      "answer": "Yes, rescue cats are often renamed. Use the new name just before food and treats, say it often in a friendly voice, and be patient. Most cats pick it up within a few weeks."
    }
  ],
